  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tulasnella violea is a small, gelatinous fungus in the Tulasnellaceae family characterized by its distinctive violet or purple coloration. This species is an important mycorrhizal partner for orchids and other plants in New England ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Violet coloration",
      "description": "Distinctive purple to violet hue on the fruiting body surfaces",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous texture",
      "description": "Soft, jelly-like consistency that becomes more apparent when moist",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Typically appears as tiny, inconspicuous fruiting bodies, often less than 5mm in diameter",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tulasnella violea is found in moist forest environments throughout New England, particularly associated with decaying wood and organic matter in shaded woodland areas.",
